  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 AM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nCurrent Conditions/Today:\n\nQuite the transition for today with summer like temperatures making \na return. An expansive upper level ridge sets up to our east as a \nsurface low pressure deepens over the Canadian Prairies. This will \nlead to a tightening pressure gradient over the region leading to \nbreezy southwest winds and broad warm air advection surging across \nthe Northland. Wind gusts will be around 35 mph and our high \ntemperatures will be nearing record breaking values in the 80s. Some \nof the CAMs are painting some weak returns in model reflecitivty \nthis afternoon across the International Border. However, we are \nkeeping PoPs below 10% as soundings show and immense dry layer \nin the low levels that rain would evaporate upon entering. This \npattern will persist through the evening hours with winds \nweakening a bit but our record warm low temperatures will likely\nbe broken as current guidance points towards overnight lows in \nthe 60s. \n\nSunday:\n\nA cold front will move slowly from northwest to southeast across our \nregion Sunday into Sunday night. The pacing of the front will be \nincredibly slow and near stationary at times. Due to this slow \nprogression high temperatures will remain very warm across most of \nthe region. Areas behind the front, namely far northern MN will see \ncooler temperatures in the 60s and low 70s. As the boundary sags \nsouth we will see rain chances slowly increase. Sounding profiles \nstill show a large amount of dry air that will need to be worked \nover before rain can reach the surface.  \n\nMonday:\n\nThe better chances for light rain arrives on Monday as a secondary \nupper level trough crosses the Upper Midwest. Lack of a deep \nmoisture source is keeping PWATs around 1.25\" and minimal \ninstability will also keep thunderstorm chances at bay. Overall rain \ntotals will be on the low side around 0.05-0.15\" With the passage of \nthe cold front we will see more seasonable temperatures return to \nthe region with highs back in the 50 and 60s.  \n\nTuesday into Midweek:\n\nHigh pressure builds into the region for Tuesday and starts to \ndepart on Wednesday. Quiet weather conditions during this \nperiod with highs in the 50s and 60s and overnight lows in the \n30s. Heading into Thursday and beyond there is a high degree of \nuncertainty among the 00Z suite of guidance. Cluster analysis \nshows a deep trough that digs into the desert southwest early \nin the week. But its progression into the midwest remains \nuncertain with a GEFS dominate clusters suggesting an active \nstretch of weather as the troughing slings through the Upper \nMidwest. But the EPS family highlights a strong ridge over the \nNorthland that would shunt the activity to our south. Will have\nto monitor to see if a better consensus emerges.",
